# Swan Compiler 2 — Lexer
#
# The lexer owns source validation, token boundaries, keyword recognition,
# literal scanning, operators, indentation and lexical diagnostics.
#
# KEYWORD POLICY
# -------------
# Keywords are intrinsic to Swan and MUST be hardcoded in this file.
# There is no keyword registry, runtime registration, configuration file,
# plugin hook, import-time keyword loading, or semantic-stage keyword rewrite.
#
# The spelling table below is the lexical source of truth for Compiler 2.
# Keep it synchronized with TokenKind and the parser grammar. A spelling may
# only be added or removed by an intentional language change with tests.
#
# Matching contract:
#   1. Scan the complete identifier first; never classify a prefix.
#   2. Compare the complete byte sequence against this exact, case-sensitive
#      table. "if" is a keyword; "iffy" and "IF" are identifiers.
#   3. Do not normalize case, Unicode, or compatibility characters.
#   4. Keywords are ASCII spellings. Non-ASCII identifier bytes remain part
#      of identifiers and can never accidentally match a keyword.
#   5. Do not allocate or hash a temporary string just to classify a keyword.
#      Compare the source slice by byte length and contents.
#   6. A failed match produces IDENT, not a lexical error.
#   7. Every listed spelling maps to exactly one token kind.
#
# HARD-CODED KEYWORD TABLE
# ------------------------
# Canonical spellings and compatibility aliases are explicit. Aliases are
# still recognized lexically; deprecation diagnostics belong to a later
# diagnostic policy, not to keyword matching.
#
# 2 bytes:
#   fn       -> FN       (compatibility spelling; retained for existing code)
#   if       -> IF
#   in       -> IN
#   as       -> AS
#
# 3 bytes:
#   let      -> LET
#   var      -> VAR
#   for      -> FOR
#   nil      -> NONE     (compatibility spelling)
#   out      -> OUT      (block terminator)
#   set      -> SET
#   and      -> AND
#   not      -> NOT
#
# 4 bytes:
#   func     -> FN       (canonical function declaration spelling)
#   else     -> ELSE
#   enum     -> ENUM
#   type     -> TYPE
#   loop     -> LOOP
#   from     -> FROM
#   self     -> SELF
#   true     -> TRUE
#   pass     -> PASS
#   null     -> NONE     (compatibility spelling)
#   bool     -> BOOL_TYPE
#   lazy     -> LAZY
#   case     -> CASE
#   or       -> OR
#   init     -> INIT
#   free     -> FREE
#   print    -> PRINT    (5 bytes; see the 5-byte group below)
#
# 5 bytes:
#   while    -> WHILE
#   match    -> MATCH
#   guard    -> GUARD
#   break    -> BREAK
#   false    -> FALSE
#   store    -> STORE    (compatibility spelling; preserve distinct token)
#   const    -> CONST
#   block    -> BLOCK
#   defer    -> DEFER
#   async    -> ASYNC
#   print    -> PRINT
#   throw    -> THROW
#
# 6 bytes:
#   manual   -> MANUAL
#   import   -> IMPORT
#   export   -> EXPORT
#   return   -> RETURN
#   struct   -> STRUCT
#   thread   -> THREAD
#   deinit   -> DEINIT
#   rethrow  -> RETHROW (7 bytes; see the 7-byte group below)
#   borrow   -> BORROW
#
# 7 bytes:
#   variant  -> VARIANT
#   rethrow  -> RETHROW
#
# 8 bytes:
#   external -> EXTERN
#   continue -> CONTINUE (8 bytes)
#   protocol -> PROTOCOL
#
# 9 bytes:
#   namespace -> NAMESPACE
#
# Operator spellings such as !, ==, ->, && and || are not identifier
# keywords. They are recognized by the operator scanner and mapped to their
# own token kinds. The words and/or/not remain keyword spellings where listed.
#
# IMPLEMENTATION REQUIREMENTS
# ---------------------------
# Implement the classifier as a length-dispatched, hardcoded decision tree
# (or an equivalent compile-time table embedded here). Check length before
# comparing bytes; this avoids prefix matches and unnecessary comparisons.
# Keep the keyword path allocation-free and deterministic.
#
# The scanner must validate UTF-8 before identifier classification. It must
# preserve byte offsets and source spans, handle a UTF-8 BOM only at byte zero,
# reject malformed UTF-8, and never use locale-dependent character routines.
# Identifier start/continuation rules must be explicit and shared by every
# identifier-scanning path.
#
# Do not silently skip unknown punctuation, malformed escapes, unterminated
# literals, inconsistent indentation, or unsupported lexical forms.
#
# IMPLEMENTATION STATUS
# ---------------------
# This file currently defines the required lexer contract and keyword
# inventory. The executable scanner and classifier are not yet implemented.
# Do not describe Compiler 2 as having a working lexer until it is implemented
# and exercised by lexer regression tests.
